  "worked_solution": "The position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=r(0) & =\u27e8-2(0),\\,{0}^{2},\\,{0}^{2}\u27e9=\u27e80,\\,0,\\,0\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =\u27e8-1,\\,0,\\,0\u27e9\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\u27e80,\\,1,\\,-1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\u27e80,\\,-1,\\,-1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (T(0)\\times N(0))=\u27e80,\\,1,\\,1\u27e9.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ \u27e8x,y,z\u27e9\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ \u27e8x,y,z\u27e9\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ \u27e8x,y,z\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80,\\,1,\\,1\u27e9 & =\u27e80,0,0\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80,\\,1,\\,1\u27e9 \\\\ 0\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+1\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ y+z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
